      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that includes all the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\ldots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients.\n\nA polynomial with rational coefficients will have as roots all the conjugates of the given roots of the form \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\). Each of these roots \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\) must have its conjugate \\(n-\\sqrt{n+1}\\) as a root. Therefore, for each root \\(n+\\sqrt{n+1}\\), the polynomial must also include the root \\(n-\\sqrt{n+1}\\).\n\nIf we have roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\ldots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), the corresponding roots for the conjugates will be \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\ldots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\). Since each original root corresponds to its conjugate, we can count the total number of roots, and thus the degree of the polynomial, by doubling the number of original roots.\n\nThere are 1000 pairs of roots \\((n + \\sqrt{n+1}, n - \\sqrt{n+1})\\). Therefore, the total number of roots and hence the degree of the polynomial is:\n\n\\[1000 \\times 2 = 2000\\]\n\nLet's verify this reasoning through Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the smallest possible degree of the polynomial\nnumber_of_terms = 1000\npolynomial_degree = number_of_terms * 2\nprint(polynomial_degree)\n```\n```output\n2000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of the polynomial with rational coefficients that includes all the given roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
