      "text": "The given simplified form is allowed by the Conjugate Root Theorem, which states that for a polynomial with rational coefficients, any irrational root must also have its conjugate as a root. For every pair of consecutive numbers \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) and \\(n + \\sqrt{n+2}\\), there are 2 additional roots: \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\) and \\(n + \\sqrt{n+2}\\), and also its conjugates \\(n - \\sqrt{n+1}\\) and \\(n - \\sqrt{n+2}\\). Therefore, there will be 2 additional roots for each pair. The total number of pairs of consecutive numbers is \\(\\frac{1000}{2} = 500\\), and thus the polynomial must have at least \\(2 \\times 500 = 1000\\) terms.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible degree of this polynomial is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\).",
