      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}, \\; 2 + \\sqrt{3}, \\; 3 + \\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients. Specifically, if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root that is an irrational number, then its conjugate must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nGiven the roots \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}, \\; 2 + \\sqrt{3}, \\; 3 + \\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\), the conjugates of these roots are \\(1 - \\sqrt{2}, \\; 2 - \\sqrt{3}, \\; 3 - \\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000 - \\sqrt{1001}\\). Therefore, to ensure that the polynomial has rational coefficients, all of these conjugates must also be roots of the polynomial.\n\nThe number of conjugate pairs is equal to the number of roots under the square root calculations, which is 1000 because there are 1000 roots of the form \\(n + \\sqrt{n+1}\\).\n\nSince each root and its conjugate form a pair, the total number of roots (and thus the degree of the polynomial) is \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\).\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning by computing the degree of the polynomial directly in Python.\n```python\nnumber_of_terms = 1000\r\ndegree_of_polynomial = number_of_terms * 2\r\nprint(degree_of_polynomial)\n```\n```output\n2000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of the polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}, \\; 2 + \\sqrt{3}, \\; 3 + \\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
